---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'State Overtime Laws Explained | overtimechecker.com';
const description = 'State-by-state overtime differences from federal law: daily overtime, double-time, and unusual exemptions.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>State Overtime Laws Explained</h1>
  <p>The federal FLSA sets a baseline that all employers must meet. States can require more &mdash; and many do. Here are the key state-by-state variations.</p>

  <h2>California (the strictest)</h2>
  <p>California has the most worker-favorable overtime rules in the country:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Daily overtime:</strong> Time-and-a-half after 8 hours in a workday</li>
    <li><strong>Weekly overtime:</strong> Time-and-a-half after 40 hours in a workweek (federal default)</li>
    <li><strong>Daily double-time:</strong> Double-time after 12 hours in a workday</li>
    <li><strong>Seventh day rule:</strong> Time-and-a-half for the first 8 hours on the 7th consecutive day; double-time after 8 hours on that day</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Alaska</h2>
  <p>Time-and-a-half after 8 hours in a workday OR 40 hours in a workweek (whichever is higher).</p>

  <h2>Nevada</h2>
  <p>Time-and-a-half after 8 hours/day, but only for employees earning less than 1.5x the state minimum wage. Higher-wage workers fall back to federal weekly rules.</p>

  <h2>Colorado</h2>
  <p>Time-and-a-half after 12 consecutive hours in a workday, OR after 40 hours in a workweek (whichever gives more).</p>

  <h2>States with industry-specific rules</h2>
  <p>Several states have special overtime rules for specific industries: agriculture (often exempt), healthcare (with alternative workweek schedules), and certain hospitality categories.</p>

  <h2>States with no daily overtime</h2>
  <p>Most states &mdash; including Texas, Florida, New York (for most workers), Illinois, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Georgia, and many others &mdash; follow the federal default: time-and-a-half after 40 hours/week with no daily threshold.</p>

  <p>For practical advice, see <a href="/know-your-rights">Know Your Rights</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
